From 392d25e131d32c04bbaa55e14f61d6fdd57e8d82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:32:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] test(desktop): send-then-close pays its cold import at
 collection and a timed-out case can't touch the next [daveey]

Problem: PR275 CI (run 41031917907, job 123983735039) failed "a message sent right before the
window closes is never lost and never sent twice" at its 30s timeout; the next case then failed
with `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'nodeType')` and the attachments case saw
"my long question" instead of "offered words".

Why 1: case 1 alone pays the file's cold module graph. Idle hub: case 1 5.6s, every other case
<0.5s; ~5s of it is the first `openPage` import (transform 4.7s). In a 1-CPU cgroup shared with
4 busy loops it ran past 30s and reproduced the CI signature exactly (timeout, then nodeType null).
Why 2: `openPage` imports after `vi.resetModules()`, so the first page's transform lands inside
the first case's timeout; earlier fixes raised the timeouts (5s -> 15s -> 30s) instead.
Why 3 (cascade): a Vitest timeout does not cancel the body. The suspended case-1 body resumed in
the next cases, whose `closeWindow()` -> `cleanup()` unmounted their page (null editor ref) and
whose `saveDraft`/send wrote "my long question" into their localStorage.
Root: helpers had no notion of which case called them, and the cold transform had no home
outside a case budget.

Fix:
- Load the composer graph once while collecting the file (no timeout), then reset modules so
  the first page still gets a fresh graph; transforms stay cached.
- Each case runs inside an AsyncLocalStorage holding Vitest's per-case abort signal; every helper
  that touches the page or storage (openPage after its imports, closeWindow, saveDraft, handles)
  throws once its own case has ended, so a stale body stops at its next step.
- New case proves the guard: a body under an ended signal renders nothing, writes no draft and
  cannot submit; red with the guard disabled.

Proof: forced suspension (case-1 page loads delayed so it times out) cascaded before
(5x6.5s: attachments case failed) and leaves every later case green after; under the same
isolated CPU stress case 1 now takes ~2.4s; 20 consecutive stressed runs pass.
